 misdiagnosed as anxiety and  depression and women are offered  antidepressants um so it's really  important that we educate gps we educate  the public so that's the reason that i  started the campaign back in 2018  is to have mandatory training for all  gps to have guidance and support in  every workplace and as you mentioned  education in schools and it's absolutely  vital it's a win-win situation both for  women  those who know and love women and also  for those who deliver health education  and it would save an absolute fortune  for the nhs too and for that yeah diane  and for those who employ women as well  of course it's good to talk to you  thanks so much indeed for joining us on  the program thank you pleasure thank you  thank you now questions have been raised  over how to protect mps after the  killing of sir david amos with  suggestions plainclothes officers or  private security could be brought in to  ensure their security stephen roberts is  joining us former deputy assistant  commissioner at the met hello she thanks  for joining us  what more can police do to protect mps  there's a whole scale of precautions  that could be taken but i think the  first point is each mp's protection  needs to be tailored to that mp  to the sort of activities that they're  undertaking when they're doing it where  they're doing it and indeed what that  particular mp is prepared to accept i i  heard uh dominique  on your program a few moments ago  saying that he would be very unhappy  with having  police officers outside a surgery that  he was running so there's going to be a  period of negotiation and proportionate  protection for each particular np  circumstances  but what about those mps that do want  support and also potentially those that  think they don't need it like i'm sure  sir david didn't feel that he needed it  and yet um he's we now he's now left you  know  he's left his widow and five daughters  well it's got to be at the end of the  day the mp themselves who agrees to the  protection uh  frankly on even on a practical scale  it's almost impossible to protect  somebody who doesn't want to be  protected but i'm sure that a  negotiation process could could get us  through that  uh and then it's a matter of there's a  scale of precautions we can start with  basically uh an appointment system to  see mps then those appointments those  people who've made appointments having  to have their identities verified  perhaps being done against intelligence  indices on those individuals and then  you're you're moving up towards physical  protection perhaps body searches knife  arches right up to the top end of the  scale but government ministers the prime  minister himself  would have a full protection  but it has to be tailored to each  individual mp and to what is sensible  and possible in in each set of  circumstances  yeah i suppose you know somebody  listening to that uh this morning who  might want to speak to their mp about  littering or  problems with one of the shops in town  or whatever if they think that their  background is going to be checked where  they live is going to be checked whether  they're on any sort of register is going  to be checked and then they're going to  have to go through an airport style  scanner and then potentially body  searched as well they're not going to do  it are they  no no and  this is part of the difficulty of  protecting the liberal democracy that we  all want to live in  and that's why i'm saying it has to be a  matter of negotiation and  ultimately it can only be provided if  the mps themselves are prepared to  accept it and you you've raised a number  of situations i can imagine situations  where somebody wants to complain about  having been victimized by the police in  themselves  and having a uniformed police officer or  knowing that there is a police officer  there would be a very off-putting  situation and  nobody would want that to be  in place no um  online hate has um according to uh the  deputy prime minister made politicians  more at risk now than they were 10 years  ago i'm guessing that you would concur  with that  oh the echo chamber of social media  can only do harm as far as i'm concerned  the idea that somebody can be  pushing hate speech towards any  individual not not just mps  but any individual  without even identifying themselves is  just  frankly absurd  um  and it needs to be rectified but equally  public discourse around politics needs  to change abuse from anonymous members  of the public is awful  but  we've seen only two recently abused from  other mps we have to learn to have  political debate with respect and that  that's a starting point that's a  cultural change that needs to happen and  that in itself  hopefully will will protect our  democracy and and our mps  stephen roberts former deputy assistant  commissioner at the metropolitan police  based in essex thank you very much  indeed for joining us thank you
 

